e37573"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-317900" :question/id #uuid "0061045b-9889-5cff-9c20-3d54327e1d3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-317900" :question/problem "Consider a system of $3$ linear equations in $3$ variables, written in matrix form as $A\\,x=b.$ Here, $A$ is the non-singular coefficient matrix of the system, $x$ is the column vector containing the variables, and $b$ is the column vector containing the constants. What are the missing parts of the derivation method shown below to find the unique solution of the system?\n\n$$\nA\\,x={{field-1}}\n$$\n\n$$\n{{field-2}}\\,x={A}^{-1}\\,b\n$$\n\n$$\n{I}_{3}\\,x={A}^{-1}\\,{{field-3}}\n$$\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}={A}^{-1}\\,b\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is not singular, it must have an inverse ${A}^{-1}.$ Now, pre-multiplying both sides of the equation $A\\,x=b$ by ${A}^{-1},$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\,x & =b \\\\ {A}^{-1}A\\,x & ={A}^{-1}\\,b \\\\ {I}_{3}\\,x & ={A}^{-1}\\,b \\\\ x & ={A}^{-1}\\,b\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-317900/field-1" "q-317900/field-2" "q-317900/field-3" "q-317900/field-4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d0cda54-4a4b-5255-b375-ce5e7b604dff"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-317900"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
